WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.104
Picture a wall of lockers at a train station, all the same width, numbered from 0.

2
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:12.711
Locker 2 holds a note from a friend: open 28.

3
00:00:12.711 --> 00:00:15.045
Locker 28 says: open 26.

4
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:16.484
You never need a map.

5
00:00:16.484 --> 00:00:21.071
Every locker is the same width, so you can count your way to any one of them.

6
00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:24.025
Now picture a payments company's fraud team.

7
00:00:24.025 --> 00:00:31.672
Their map shows who is linked to whom: accounts, the phones and devices they log in from, and the money they send each other.

8
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:36.067
It finds the close links of a suspicious account in a millisecond.

9
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:39.249
Next year the map will be a thousand times bigger.

10
00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:42.559
Will the same question take a thousand times longer?

11
00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:43.959
Most people guess yes.

12
00:00:43.959 --> 00:00:44.977
Hold that guess.

13
00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:47.707
A map like this is called a graph.

14
00:00:47.707 --> 00:00:52.436
Each thing is a node: 13 accounts, 9 devices and 6 phone numbers.

15
00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:55.372
Each link is a relationship, with a type.

16
00:00:55.372 --> 00:00:59.046
USES, when an account logs in from a phone or a device.

17
00:00:59.046 --> 00:01:02.787
TRANSFERRED_TO, when one account sends money to another.

18
00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:11.347
The fraud team's question is a walk: start at account A-3101, follow its relationships to its neighbours, then theirs.

19
00:01:11.347 --> 00:01:14.329
Each step along one relationship is a hop.

20
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:21.808
The obvious way to store this is a table of links, one row per relationship, with the ids of its two ends.

21
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:32.429
To find A-3101's links, the database searches that table with an index: a sorted tree of pages, walked from the top down, one page per level.

22
00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:35.759
At 100,000 rows the tree is 3 levels deep.

23
00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:37.025
At 10 million, 4.

24
00:01:37.025 --> 00:01:42.979
So every hop costs a search, and the search grows, slowly, with the whole graph.

25
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:47.547
What we'd like is the lockers: go straight to the next link, without searching.

26
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:52.848
Neo4j, a database program built to store graphs, does exactly that.

27
00:01:52.848 --> 00:01:57.788
Every record in a file is the same size, and has a number, its id.

28
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.995
A node record is 15 bytes, so node N starts at byte N times 15.

29
00:02:02.995 --> 00:02:09.258
A relationship record is 34 bytes, so relationship N starts at byte N times 34.

30
00:02:09.258 --> 00:02:11.716
Finding a record is arithmetic.

31
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:20.568
Let's map it: the files are the wall of lockers, a record's id is the locker's number, and the pointer inside a record is the note.

32
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.346
A-3101 is node 2.

33
00:02:22.346 --> 00:02:28.347
How many records must Neo4j read to find all of its relationships?

34
00:02:28.347 --> 00:02:29.801
Pause and guess.

35
00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:35.488
Start at byte 2 times 15: 30.

36
00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:39.297
The node record says: first relationship, 28.

37
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:51.304
Byte 28 times 34, 952: A-3104 sent money to A-3101, and its note for A-3101 says next, 26.

38
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:57.150
Byte 884: A-3101 sent money to A-3103.

39
00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.340
Next, 5.

40
00:02:58.567 --> 00:03:04.897
Byte 170: A-3101 uses the phone P-0177.

41
00:03:04.897 --> 00:03:06.196
Next, 4.

42
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:15.217
Byte 136: A-3101 uses the device D-77, and its next says minus one: the end.

43
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.414
Five reads, four relationships, and nothing was searched.

44
00:03:19.414 --> 00:03:24.203
The count depends on how many links A-3101 has, and on nothing else.

45
00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:26.466
Notice the order: newest first.

46
00:03:26.466 --> 00:03:31.627
Each new relationship goes in at the front of its chain, like a new note on top.

47
00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:36.543
And each one points back as well as forward, so the chain is doubly linked.

48
00:03:36.543 --> 00:03:39.996
A relationship belongs to two chains, one for each end.

49
00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:50.605
Relationship 5 also carries on along P-0177's chain, to relationship 7: A-3102 uses the same phone.

50
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:57.818
Crossing to a neighbour is the same move: P-0177 is node 24, at byte 360.

51
00:03:57.818 --> 00:04:00.605
One multiplication, one read.

52
00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:07.301
Neo4j calls this index-free adjacency: finding a node's neighbours without an index.

53
00:04:07.567 --> 00:04:10.479
Here's where the lockers stop working.

54
00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:15.307
Walking to locker 30,000 takes longer than walking to locker 3.

55
00:04:15.307 --> 00:04:19.981
But 30,000 times 34 is no slower to work out than 3 times 34.

56
00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:22.111
The lockers give you tired legs.

57
00:04:22.111 --> 00:04:23.752
The multiplication doesn't.

58
00:04:23.752 --> 00:04:27.824
What takes time is reading the record, and that's the next section.

59
00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:30.815
And why doesn't every database do this?

60
00:04:30.815 --> 00:04:34.309
It only works when every record is the same size.

61
00:04:34.309 --> 00:04:37.020
A shop's name may be 3 letters or 300.

62
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.012
In Neo4j, a long string spills into more records and costs more reads.

63
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:53.855
And an id says nothing about what's inside: to find the account called A-3101 in the first place, Neo4j still searches a tree, once, at the start of the walk.

64
00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.938
So, a thousand times bigger, a thousand times slower?

65
00:04:57.938 --> 00:04:59.169
Not for the hops.

66
00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:02.296
Record N lives at byte N times its size.

67
00:05:02.296 --> 00:05:05.225
Find the first node once, then multiply.

68
00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:06.698
One quick check.

69
00:05:06.698 --> 00:05:09.162
Where does relationship 7 start?

70
00:05:09.162 --> 00:05:10.393
Pause and think.

71
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.081
7 times 34: byte 238.

72
00:05:16.081 --> 00:05:18.797
No search, at any size.
